\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Configuration}

Our primary experiments use GPT2-125M (124M parameters) trained on WikiText-103 (232K training samples). We run 8 self-training generations (Generation~0 through~7). At each generation, the current model generates 500K tokens via nucleus sampling (temperature~$0.8$, top-$p$~$0.95$) conditioned on WikiText-103 prefix prompts, a data selection method curates a subset, and the model is fine-tuned on the result.

We compare four data selection methods spanning a factorial ablation design:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RGDS} (50\%): diversity selection using the training model's own last-hidden-layer embeddings.
    \item \textbf{External-only} (50\%): the same selection algorithm and budget, using frozen Contriever \citep{izacard2022unsupervised} embeddings that are never updated during training.
    \item \textbf{Random 50\%}: uniform random selection with the same data budget.
    \item \textbf{Uncurated} (100\%): all generated data retained without selection.
\end{itemize}

All experiments use 3 random seeds (42, 123, 456). Within each seed, all methods start from the same pretrained checkpoint and share the same Generation~0 data pool. For External-only and RGDS, two of three seeds are complete at time of writing.

\subsection{Training Details}

Each generation fine-tunes from the previous generation's checkpoint using AdamW with learning rate $5 \times 10^{-5}$ and linear warmup, batch size~4 with gradient accumulation over 4~steps (effective batch size~16), maximum sequence length~512, and 3~epochs per generation. Generation~0 starts from the HuggingFace pretrained GPT2-125M checkpoint.

\subsection{Selection Algorithm}

Both RGDS and External-only apply the same greedy selection procedure. Embeddings (768-dimensional for both GPT2 and Contriever) are projected to 32 dimensions via PCA fitted on the WikiText-103 training set. The algorithm iteratively selects the candidate whose inclusion most reduces the normalized eigenvalue variance of the selected subset's covariance matrix:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(S) = \frac{\lVert \mathbf{C}(S) \rVert_F}{\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}(S))^2}
\label{eq:selection_objective}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{C}(S)$ is the covariance of the selected set. This objective favors subsets with uniform eigenvalues, selecting for maximal dimensional coverage in the projected embedding space. Unlike facility location objectives that maximize minimum pairwise distance between selected points, eigenvalue variance minimization operates on the spectral properties of the entire subset, promoting distributional coverage rather than point-wise separation.

The two methods differ only in embedding source: RGDS uses the current-generation model's last-hidden-layer representations, which co-evolve with training; External-only uses a frozen Contriever encoder.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We evaluate each method at every generation along five dimensions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Perplexity (PPL)}: language modeling quality on the WikiText-103 validation set.
    \item \textbf{Selection ratio} ($\text{SR}_g$): ratio of mean rare trigram density in the selected subset to that in the full generated pool, quantifying alignment between the selection criterion and content diversity.
    \item \textbf{Rare token ratio}: proportion of generated trigrams at or below the 10th percentile of the WikiText-103 trigram frequency distribution, measuring lexical tail preservation.
    \item \textbf{RankMe}: effective embedding rank via singular value entropy \citep{garrido2023rankme}, tracking representation dimensionality.
    \item \textbf{Rank correlation}: Spearman correlation between per-sample embedding diversity scores and rare token content, measuring sample-level alignment between the selection criterion and content diversity.
\end{itemize}

Generation~7 summary statistics appear in Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}; per-generation perplexity trajectories are plotted in Figure~\ref{fig:ppl_trends}.

\subsection{Scaling Experiment: GPT2-Medium}
\label{sec:scaling}

% TODO: GPT2-medium 355M 结果待补
To assess whether the embedding alignment gap generalizes beyond 125M parameters, we replicate the full experimental protocol with GPT2-Medium (355M parameters), using identical training configuration, data selection methods, and evaluation metrics.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary} summarizes Generation~7 metrics across all methods and seeds. Figure~\ref{fig:ppl_trends} shows per-generation perplexity trajectories. We organize findings around three primary claims and one directional observation.

\subsection{RGDS Produces Catastrophic Perplexity Degradation}
\label{sec:result_c1}

RGDS is the only method that exhibits catastrophic performance degradation across generations. At Generation~7, RGDS reaches a perplexity of $78.32 \pm 4.60$ ($n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}), compared to $47.04 \pm 2.90$ for Uncurated ($n{=}3$; +66\%) and $43.18 \pm 1.43$ for Random~50\% ($n{=}3$; +82\%). Both completed seeds show the same pattern: 75.07 (seed=42) and 81.57 (seed=123), with effect sizes that exceed inter-seed variance within any other method by an order of magnitude.

The selection ratio traces the mechanism behind this degradation. RGDS's selection ratio follows an overall declining trend from ${\sim}1.04$ at Generation~0 to $0.52 \pm 0.004$ at Generation~7, with a transient rebound during Generations~1--3 ($0.91 \to 0.93 \to 0.94$) before sustained decline from Generation~4 onward. By the final generation, RGDS preferentially retains vocabulary-poor samples: its selected subsets contain approximately half the rare trigram density of the full generated pool. The model's internal notion of ``diverse'' has inverted relative to content diversity.

% TODO: seed=456 RGDS 完成后更新为 n=3 mean±std 并补充统计检验 p 值

\subsection{External Embeddings Avoid the Alignment Gap}
\label{sec:result_c2}

External-only selection applies the identical selection algorithm and 50\% data budget but uses frozen Contriever embeddings. It avoids catastrophic degradation entirely: its Generation~7 perplexity of $47.59 \pm 2.93$ ($n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}) falls within the range of Uncurated ($47.04 \pm 2.90$) and Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$).

External-only maintains a selection ratio above~1.0 throughout all generations ($1.11 \pm 0.02$ at Generation~7), indicating continued preferential retention of content-diverse samples. Its rank correlation between diversity scores and rare token content remains weakly positive ($+0.011 \pm 0.000$; Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}), while RGDS's correlation turns negative ($-0.035 \pm 0.005$).

Since RGDS and External-only differ in exactly one variable---the embedding source---the 30.7-point perplexity gap at Generation~7 ($78.32$ vs.\ $47.59$) is directly attributable to the use of co-degrading internal representations. The selection algorithm is not at fault; the failure is specific to coupling data selection to embeddings that co-evolve with the collapsing model.

% TODO: seed=456 ext/rgds 完成后更新为 n=3 mean±std 并补充 RGDS vs External t 检验

\subsection{Internal Selection Underperforms Random Baseline}
\label{sec:result_c3}

The comparison between RGDS and Random~50\% provides the cleanest controlled ablation: both use the same 50\% data budget, isolating the effect of the selection mechanism itself. RGDS's Generation~7 perplexity ($78.32 \pm 4.60$, $n{=}2$\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}) exceeds Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$, $n{=}3$) by 82\%. Internal embedding diversity selection does not merely fail to improve upon random sampling---it actively degrades model quality relative to uninformed selection.

Rare token ratio confirms this finding: RGDS retains $0.0057 \pm 0.0008$ at Generation~7, compared to $0.0098 \pm 0.0003$ for Random~50\%---a 42\% reduction in lexical tail content.

\subsection{Random Pruning: A Directional Observation}
\label{sec:result_s3}

Random~50\% ($43.18 \pm 1.43$, $n{=}3$) achieves lower Generation~7 perplexity than Uncurated ($47.04 \pm 2.90$, $n{=}3$), a reduction of 8.2\%. The direction is consistent across all three seeds. However, this difference does not reach conventional significance ($p \approx 0.10$, two-sided $t$-test), and we report it as a directional observation rather than a confirmed finding.

If confirmed with additional statistical power, this result would suggest that training on a random 50\% subset provides mild regularization compared to using all generated data in the iterative self-training setting, consistent with observations that data pruning can improve generalization even without informed selection \citep{sorscher2022beyond}.

\subsection{Lexical Diversity Collapse Across Methods}
\label{sec:result_diversity}

RGDS exhibits the sharpest decline in rare token ratio, dropping from approximately 0.010 at Generation~0 to $0.0057 \pm 0.0008$ at Generation~7 (Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}). The remaining methods converge to a narrower band: Random~50\% ($0.0098 \pm 0.0003$), Uncurated ($0.0090 \pm 0.0004$), and External-only ($0.0102 \pm 0.0004$). Relative to the WikiText-103 baseline rare token ratio of approximately 0.029, all non-RGDS methods exhibit roughly 65\% lexical tail erosion by Generation~7; RGDS reaches approximately 80\%.

This pattern indicates that lexical diversity collapse is primarily driven by the iterative self-training loop itself \citep{shumailov2024ai}, not by the choice of data selection method. RGDS is distinctive in accelerating this inherent degradation; the remaining methods neither prevent nor substantially exacerbate it.

\subsection{Representation Metrics Do Not Track Content Collapse}
\label{sec:result_rankme}

RankMe values at Generation~7 are comparable across all methods (289--296; Table~\ref{tab:gen7_summary}), with RGDS showing a slightly elevated value ($295.54 \pm 3.98$) relative to the other methods (${\sim}290$). The metric designed to track effective embedding dimensionality does not distinguish between catastrophically degraded RGDS and the normally performing alternatives, indicating that embedding structural properties can persist even as the space loses content-discriminative fidelity.

